---
/**
 * Accessibility statement (Task 200). It states a target (WCAG 2.2 AA, the level the browser tests check with axe,
 * docs/ACCESSIBILITY.md) and the commitments CLAUDE.md rule 6 and D-48 already make, and it never claims a
 * conformance certification: automated checks are not an audit. Not legal advice; attorney review pending.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import LegalApproval from '../components/LegalApproval.astro';
import { isLegalPageApproved } from '../config/legalapproval';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';
---

<Layout title="accessibility" description="How Cueory works for everyone, what we test, and how to tell us when it doesn't." noindex={!isLegalPageApproved("accessibility")}>
  <Prose>
    <h1>accessibility</h1>
    <LegalApproval page="accessibility" />

    <h2>Our goal</h2>
    <p>
      We want every nursing student to be able to use {site.name}, whatever device or assistive technology they use.
      Our target is the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 at level AA. We are not there on every page yet,
      and we don't claim a certified conformance level; this page says what we do and how to reach us when something
      is in the way.
    </p>

    <h2>What we build in</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Everything you can drag you can also do by tapping or with the keyboard.</li>
      <li>The keyboard focus is always visible, and the tab order follows the page.</li>
      <li>Right and wrong are never shown by colour alone; a word or a symbol always goes with it.</li>
      <li>Text colours meet WCAG AA contrast against every background they sit on, in both the light and dark looks.</li>
      <li>Animation and motion are reduced or removed when your device asks for reduced motion.</li>
      <li>Text reflows when you enlarge it, and no page scrolls sideways on a phone, a tablet or a desktop.</li>
      <li>Buttons are real buttons with real names, and corner buttons are at least 44 by 44 pixels.</li>
      <li>Every page has a skip link, a single main heading, headings in order and a page language.</li>
      <li>Lessons and study tools keep working without JavaScript where they can, or say plainly when they can't.</li>
      <li>Light and dark looks follow your device, and you can override them at the bottom of any page.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How we check</h2>
    <p>
      These are targets we test for, not a certified result. On every pull request that changes the site, automated
      browser tests run the axe accessibility rules for WCAG 2.0 A and AA, 2.1 AA and 2.2 AA on a representative set of
      pages at phone and desktop sizes, with further checks for reduced motion and enlarged text, and a high-contrast
      check of the home page; before a release to the live site they also run at tablet size, in the dark look on the
      pages covered by the dark-mode audit, and in Safari's and Firefox's engines.
      Changes that touch only documentation skip them. Automated checks catch many problems but not all of them: manual
      checks with a keyboard and with assistive technology are still needed, and the site has not yet had a full audit
      by an independent accessibility specialist.
    </p>

    <h2>Known limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Lesson videos play in YouTube's player, whose controls and captions are YouTube's.</li>
      <li>The 3D anatomy lab has a text alternative for its structures, but a 3D scene itself can't be fully described in words.</li>
      <li>Some lessons are samples or drafts and may still have gaps we haven't found.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Tell us</h2>
    <p>
      If a page is hard to use, email <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a> or use the "Flag
      or comment" button at the bottom of the page. Tell us the page, what you were trying to do, and the device,
      browser and assistive technology you were using. We read every report and aim to reply within ten business days.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</Layout>
